---
title: Your first C project
description: Create a C17 application, add a source/header pair, run its tests, and publish API documentation with Doxygen.
section: Start here
order: 5
updated: '2026-10-13'
---

Build a small application named `counter` with a reusable C library and a CTest test. Then add a function and document its public interface with Doxygen. Rivet creates an ordinary CMake project; you can keep using its files outside Neovim.

## Check your tools

[Install Rivet](/docs/start-here/#install-in-neovim) in Neovim 0.11 or newer. This walkthrough needs CMake 3.30+, Ninja, and a C17 compiler available to CMake. Doxygen and clang-format are optional additions. After creating the project, `:RivetDoctor` checks its tools.

## Create the project

Open Neovim in a new directory:

```sh
mkdir counter
cd counter
nvim
```

Run `:RivetNew`. If you see a profile picker, choose **New choices**. Use these answers:

| Prompt               | Choice                                                                |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Project language     | `c`                                                                   |
| Create project in    | Current folder                                                        |
| Project name         | `counter`                                                             |
| Git repository       | Skip Git initialization, or initialize/reuse a repository             |
| Project license      | No license yet, or your chosen license with its requested holder/year |
| File comment headers | Personalize and preview styles                                        |
| Header style         | Doxygen documentation                                                 |
| Project kind         | Application                                                           |
| C standard           | C17                                                                   |
| C header protection  | `#ifndef / #define`                                                   |

Header identity fields are optional. **Doxygen documentation** creates file comments with Doxygen tags; add function comments as your API grows. The [header guide](/docs/comment-headers/) explains how to change this choice later.

Review the files and press `c` or Ctrl-S to apply, or `q` to cancel. The project goes directly into `counter`, with no second nested directory. Creation checks for collisions before writing files. Selecting Git does not stage or commit them.

The important files are `app/main.c`, `src/core.c`, `include/counter.h`, and `tests/core_test.c`. The `counter_core` static library supplies `rivet_add`; the `counter` application and `counter_test` executable link to it.

## Build, run, and test

```vim
:edit app/main.c
:RivetTask build
```

Choose **debug** when asked for a preset. Rivet configures CMake and builds the project. Run `:RivetTask run`; the application prints:

```text
42
```

Run `:RivetTask test`. CTest should report one passing test, `counter_test`. It checks positive and negative operands without an external test framework. Use `q` to close the output, `:RivetOutput` to reopen it, and `:RivetPreset` to change the remembered preset.

## Add a source/header pair

Run `:RivetFile`, select **c**, then **C source and header**. Enter `answer` and select **counter_core** as the owning target. The preview adds `src/answer.c` and `include/answer.h`, and updates the target registration. Apply it with `c`.

In `include/answer.h`, keep the generated guard and C++ compatibility wrapper. Replace `void answer(void);` with:

```c
/** @brief Return the application's example answer. */
int answer(void);
```

Replace the function in `src/answer.c`, keeping its include and optional file header:

```c
#include "answer.h"

int answer(void) { return 42; }
```

In `app/main.c`, include `answer.h` and change the printed expression from `rivet_add(20, 22)` to `answer()`. Save with `:wall` and run `:RivetTask run` again. The result remains `42`; the application now calls your new library function.

For a nested component, enter a project-relative name such as `src/io/message` in the file wizard. This creates the missing parent directories and places both files together as `src/io/message.c` and `src/io/message.h`. Review the paths and target before applying. A bare name uses the normal `src/` and `include/` locations. See [Files and targets](/docs/templates/) for other arrangements.

## Generate API documentation with Doxygen

Doxygen is a useful next step for a C library: keep parameter descriptions, return values, and preconditions beside the declarations callers use. Rivet's **Doxygen documentation** header supplies file-level context; Doxygen generates the browsable reference. Install Doxygen separately, then run this from the project root if you do not already have a `Doxyfile`:

```sh
doxygen -g Doxyfile
```

Edit these settings in that file:

```text
PROJECT_NAME = counter
INPUT = include src
FILE_PATTERNS = *.h *.c
RECURSIVE = YES
OPTIMIZE_OUTPUT_FOR_C = YES
OUTPUT_DIRECTORY = docs/api
GENERATE_HTML = YES
GENERATE_LATEX = NO
```

Add an `@file` comment to a header if it does not already have one, and document each public function. For example, above `rivet_add` in `include/counter.h`:

```c
/**
 * @brief Add two integers.
 * @param a First operand.
 * @param b Second operand.
 * @pre The mathematical sum is representable as an int.
 * @return The sum of a and b.
 */
int rivet_add(int a, int b);
```

Create the output directory and generate the reference:

```sh
mkdir -p docs/api
doxygen Doxyfile
```

Then open `docs/api/html/index.html` in your browser. Add `/docs/api/` to `.gitignore` if you keep generated documentation out of source control. These commands run Doxygen directly; Rivet does not install it or generate a Doxyfile automatically. The official [getting-started guide](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/starting.html), [comment syntax](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/docblocks.html), and [C output setting](https://www.doxygen.nl/manual/config.html#cfg_optimize_output_for_c) explain the configuration.

## Keep iterating

Save your edits before tasks. A compiler failure appears in the output; `:copen` opens collected diagnostics and Enter jumps to the selected location. Fix the error and use `:RivetTask repeat`. `:RivetConfig clang-format` reviews formatting settings, and the [C guide](/docs/c/) covers native project ownership and future targets.
